{"title": "Sandbox", "description": "Plan, review, approve, and run sandbox scripts, including pre-approved scheduled execution.", "examples": ["automate this with a script", "set up a recurring script run"], "events": ["scheduled.script"], "tools": ["command_execute", "development_session_request", "network_access_request", "script_approval_request", "script_development_review"]}

Runs commands in an isolated sandbox container. Outbound network access is deny-by-default: traffic goes through an HTTPS-only proxy with script-bound host permissions. For iterative work, call development_session_request with an objective, data access, external effects, hosts and limits. After admin approval, call script_development_review for each changed version or host set before execution. Submit script_approval_request with the final exact command and complete host list for combined approval. Grants cover entire HTTPS hosts, not URL paths. Never work around the proxy (other hosts, mirrors, IP addresses, DNS tricks, or unproxied clients), and never use the sandbox to replace a dedicated tool that failed; ask the administrator instead. Application credentials are not available here. Never search environment variables or secret files for API keys, or recreate authenticated integrations; use their dedicated tools. Use runtime_capabilities for live modes and help_search for sandbox restrictions and packages.

Only unchanged approved versions may be rerun within their session limits. Final approval closes development authorization and binds the code and dependency hashes, invocation, image and policy. A one-time approval permits one execution even if it fails. Approved code runs from a read-only snapshot; use the working directory or explicit workspace paths for output. Explicitly configured YOLO mode skips script approval gates, not sandbox restrictions.

Before task_complete, finalize every open development session, not just the most recent script. Submit its final exact command and hosts, then wait for the runtime's approval decision. A pending approval is a pause, not a task failure. Do not rerun finished work or create another development session merely to clear finalization.

For future or recurring sandbox work, write and test the script now. Use one reminder_create with execution='script' to reserve a one-off execution or obtain standing approval for recurring runs; never defer code preparation or approval to an agent-task reminder. Within a development session, this scheduling call is the final approval request. Specify the follow-up work in the task text. At execution, use the approved scheduled binding rather than creating a new approval or changing the invocation.
